 Neural Control of Gyrokinetic Turbulence in Stellarators

Toward Real‑Time Optimization of Fusion Plasma Confinement

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Date: April 2026

---

Abstract

Magnetic confinement fusion in stellarators has achieved significant progress through devices like Wendelstein 7‑X, which demonstrate dramatically reduced neoclassical transport. However, plasma confinement is now fundamentally limited by nonlinear gyrokinetic turbulence. Recent work has shown that stellarator configurations can be optimized using direct nonlinear gyrokinetic simulations rather than geometric proxies. Building on this paradigm, we propose the Neural Turbulence‑Controlled Stellarator (NTCS) framework. NTCS reformulates stellarator optimization as a stochastic optimal control problem over a learned latent dynamical system, integrating GPU‑accelerated gyrokinetic simulations (GX), neural operator surrogates with structure‑preserving constraints, and dual‑mode model predictive control with conformal safety filters. Three novel physics‑grounded control objectives are introduced: zonal‑flow amplification, critical‑gradient maximization, and turbulence‑structure targeting, each derived from gyrokinetic predator‑prey theory. The paper provides rigorous mathematical formulations, explicit assumptions, architectural specifications, and a validated reduced model. NTCS outlines a realistic pathway toward adaptive, learning‑based fusion devices that can control turbulence in real time.

Keywords: stellarator, gyrokinetic turbulence, neural operators, optimal control, predator‑prey model, zonal flows, model predictive control, conformal prediction, structure‑preserving machine learning

