Assumptions (Explicit)

Before developing the framework, we state the assumptions under which the NTCS formulation is valid:

1. Lipschitz continuity: The latent dynamics G_\phi and the cost function L are L_G- and L_L-Lipschitz in their arguments on the domain of operation.
2. Bounded process noise: The latent state evolves with additive Gaussian noise \boldsymbol{\xi}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma) with \|\Sigma\| \leq \sigma_{\max} < \infty.
3. Actuator constraints: The control signal \mathbf{a}(t) belongs to a compact, convex set \mathcal{A} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n_a} with finite bandwidth \omega_{\text{act}}.
4. Timescale separation: The turbulence autocorrelation time \tau_{\text{corr}} \sim 10{-}100\,\mu\text{s} is well‑separated from the actuator response time \tau_{\text{act}} \sim 1{-}10\,\text{ms} and the profile evolution time \tau_{\text{prof}} \sim 100\,\text{ms}, ensuring a hierarchical control structure.
5. Nominal magnetic geometry: The base stellarator field \mathbf{B}_{\text{base}} is fixed by the superconducting coils; control acts through perturbations to heating deposition, the radial electric field E_r, and error‑field compensation, not through real‑time 3D geometry deformation.
6. Surrogate fidelity: There exists \varepsilon > 0 such that \|G_\phi(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{a}) - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{a})\|_\infty \leq \varepsilon on the training distribution, where \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}} is the true projected gyrokinetic operator.

