5. Control Objectives: Formal Derivation

5.1 Zonal‑Flow Amplification

From the Lotka–Volterra model (3), the fixed point (E^*, U^*) = (\gamma/\sigma, \alpha/\beta) determines the steady‑state turbulence level. The zonal‑flow energy is:

E_{\text{ZF}} = \int |\langle\tilde{\phi}\rangle_{\text{flux surface}}|^2 dV.

Proposition 1 (Zonal Flow Regulation, Stability Claim). Under Assumptions (1)–(4), maximizing E_{\text{ZF}} is equivalent to minimizing the effective turbulent diffusivity D_{\text{turb}} \propto \langle \tilde{v}_r^2 \rangle / (1 + (\omega_{E\times B}/\gamma_{\text{lin}})^2), provided the zonal‑flow shearing rate \omega_{E\times B} does not exceed the actuator bandwidth \omega_{\text{act}}. The control‑augmented Lotka–Volterra dynamics read:

\frac{dE}{dt} = \alpha E - \beta(\mathbf{a}) E U, \qquad
\frac{dU}{dt} = -\gamma U + \sigma E U + \kappa a_{\text{ZF}}, \tag{8}

where \beta = \beta_0 + \beta_1 a_{\text{ZF}} and \kappa > 0 is the direct zonal‑flow drive efficiency. The control action a_{\text{ZF}} enters through ECRH deposition shaping that modifies the radial electric field shear.

Objective term: L_{\text{ZF}} = -\alpha_{\text{ZF}} E_{\text{ZF}}(\mathbf{z}).

5.2 Critical Gradient Maximization

The critical gradient \nabla T_{\text{crit}} is the threshold above which ITG modes are destabilized. Roberg-Clark et al. demonstrated that ITG stability can be improved by manipulating magnetic geometry to enhance the peak drift curvature frequency, and that configurations optimized for turbulence above the critical gradient achieve heat fluxes below or comparable to W7-X high‑mirror [9].

Proposition 2 (Critical Gradient Control). For localized toroidal ITG modes in a quasi‑isodynamic configuration, \nabla T_{\text{crit}} \approx C_0 |\omega_{\text{drift}} / (v_{\text{th},i} / L_\parallel)|, where L_\parallel is the parallel correlation length set by average curvature. Actuator perturbations can increase \nabla T_{\text{crit}} by adjusting the drift curvature well depth, as shown through linear gyrokinetic analysis. The control signal enters through trim coil currents a_{\text{trim}} that modify the local magnetic shear, and ECRH deposition that shapes the temperature profile.

Objective term: L_{\text{crit}} = -\beta_{\text{crit}} \nabla T_{\text{crit}}(\mathbf{z}; \mathbf{a}).

5.3 Turbulence Structure Targeting

We define a reference latent vector \mathbf{z}_{\text{ref}} corresponding to a low‑transport nonlinear attractor identified from GX simulation data (e.g., a zonal‑flow‑dominated staircase with minimal radial transport).

Objective term: L_{\text{struct}} = \gamma_{\text{struct}} \|\mathbf{z} - \mathbf{z}_{\text{ref}}\|^2.

5.4 Combined Lagrangian

L(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a}) = -\alpha_{\text{ZF}} E_{\text{ZF}}(\mathbf{z}) - \beta_{\text{crit}} \nabla T_{\text{crit}}(\mathbf{z}; \mathbf{a}) + \gamma_{\text{struct}} \|\mathbf{z} - \mathbf{z}_{\text{ref}}\|^2 + \delta Q_{\text{turb}}(\mathbf{z}). \tag{9}

