7. Theoretical Guarantees (Revised)

7.1 Regret Bound

Theorem 1 (Sublinear Regret of NTCS‑MPC, under Assumptions). Under Assumptions (1)–(6), with surrogate error \|G_\phi - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}\|_\infty \leq \varepsilon and horizon H, the MPC policy achieves:

\mathcal{R}_T(\pi^{\text{MPC}}) \leq \mathcal{O}(T \cdot L_L \cdot \varepsilon) + \mathcal{O}\left( \frac{T}{H} \right).

Proof sketch. The first term arises from error accumulation \varepsilon over T steps via the Lipschitz property of L. The second term is the standard receding‑horizon suboptimality bound. As \varepsilon \to 0 with increasing training data and H grows, the per‑step regret vanishes. ∎

7.2 Practical Stability Claim

Proposition 3 (Practical Stability of Controlled Zonal‑Flow Dynamics). Under Assumptions (1)–(5), with the zonal‑flow‑amplifying objective \alpha_{\text{ZF}} > 0 and bounded actuator response \|a_{\text{ZF}}\| \leq a_{\max}, the control‑augmented predator‑prey system (8) possesses a globally attractive compact set \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2. Specifically:

· If \sigma E^* > \gamma (above the zonal‑flow damping threshold), the system converges to a limit cycle.
· If \sigma E^* \leq \gamma, the system converges to the fixed point (E^*, U^*) = (\gamma/\sigma, \alpha/\beta(\mathbf{a})).

The zonal‑flow energy U is bounded below by \kappa a_{\text{ZF}} / \gamma in steady state, ensuring a minimum level of turbulence suppression under any admissible control.

Proof. Standard Lyapunov analysis of the augmented Lotka–Volterra system (8); the proof follows from the boundedness of \beta(\mathbf{a}) and a_{\text{ZF}} (Assumption 3). We emphasise that this is a stability claim under stated assumptions rather than a rigorous theorem for the full PDE system; see Assumptions (1)–(5) for the scope. ∎

