Appendix A — Full Gyrokinetic Derivation

A.1 Gyrokinetic Vlasov–Maxwell System

Starting point is the Hamiltonian for a single particle in an electromagnetic field:

H = \tfrac{1}{2} m v^2 + e \Phi,
\tag{A.1}

where \Phi is the scalar potential and the vector potential \mathbf{A} enters through the canonical momentum. After performing the Lie‑transform perturbation theory to decouple the fast gyromotion, the gyrocenter Hamiltonian becomes

\bar{H} = \tfrac{1}{2} m v_\parallel^2 + \mu B + e\langle \phi \rangle - \frac{m}{2B^2} |\nabla_\perp \phi|^2 + \cdots,
\tag{A.2}

where \mu = m v_\perp^2 / (2B) is the magnetic moment (an adiabatic invariant), \langle \phi \rangle is the gyroaveraged electrostatic potential, and the last term is the second‑order ponderomotive potential. The gyrocenter equations of motion are Hamiltonian:

\dot{\mathbf{R}} = \{\mathbf{R}, \bar{H}\},
\qquad
\dot{v}_\parallel = \{v_\parallel, \bar{H}\},
\tag{A.3}

with the gyrocenter Poisson bracket defined in terms of the symplectic 2‑form \omega. The gyrocenter phase‑space volume is preserved (Liouville theorem) with the Jacobian \mathcal{J} = B^*_\parallel / m, where B^*_\parallel is the parallel component of the effective magnetic field.

A.2 Variational Principle and Noether Conservation Laws

The gyrokinetic Vlasov–Maxwell equations can be derived from a Lagrangian density written in Eulerian–Poincaré form:

\mathcal{L} = \int d^6 Z \, \bar{f} \left[ \frac{e}{c} \mathbf{A}^* \cdot \dot{\mathbf{R}} + \frac{mc}{e} \mu \dot{\theta} - \bar{H} \right] + \mathcal{L}_{\text{field}},
\tag{A.4}

where \mathbf{A}^* = \mathbf{A} + (m c / e) v_\parallel \mathbf{b} is the modified vector potential and \theta is the gyroangle. Variation of the action with respect to \bar{f}, \phi, and A_\parallel yields the gyrokinetic Vlasov equation, the gyrokinetic Poisson equation, and the parallel Ampère equation respectively.

Theorem A.1 (Noether energy conservation). The gyrokinetic Vlasov–Maxwell system possesses an exact energy conservation law derived by the Noether method from the variational principle:

\frac{dE_{\text{tot}}}{dt} = 0, \qquad
E_{\text{tot}} = \int d^6 Z \, \bar{H} \bar{f} + \frac{1}{8\pi} \int d^3 x \, \left(|\nabla_\perp \phi|^2 + |\nabla_\perp A_\parallel|^2 \right).
\tag{A.5}

Proof sketch. The Lagrangian (A.4) is invariant under time translations. Applying Noether’s theorem yields the local energy conservation law \partial_t \mathcal{E} + \nabla \cdot \mathbf{S} = 0. Integration over the plasma volume yields (A.5). The detailed derivation is given in Brizard (2017) and Brizard & Hahm (2007).

A.3 Gyrokinetic Equation in Pseudospectral Form

The δf gyrokinetic equation in the drift‑kinetic limit reads:

\frac{\partial g}{\partial t} + v_\parallel \mathbf{b} \cdot \nabla g + \mathbf{v}_d \cdot \nabla g + \dot{v}_\parallel \frac{\partial g}{\partial v_\parallel} = C[g] - \frac{q}{T} F_0 \frac{\partial \langle \phi \rangle}{\partial t},
\tag{A.6}

where

\dot{v}_\parallel = -\frac{1}{m} \left( \mathbf{b} + \frac{m v_\parallel}{q B} \nabla \times \mathbf{b} \right) \cdot \left( \mu \nabla B + q \nabla \langle \phi \rangle \right),
\qquad
\mathbf{v}_d = \frac{1}{B^2} \left( \mu B + m v_\parallel^2 \right) \mathbf{b} \times \nabla B + \frac{m v_\parallel^2}{q B} \nabla \times \mathbf{b}.

The GX code uses a pseudospectral discretisation in all phase‑space dimensions: Fourier in the configuration space (x, y), Laguerre in \mu, and Hermite in v_\parallel. The gyroaveraging operator is applied spectrally using Bessel functions: in Fourier space, \langle \phi \rangle_k = J_0(k_\perp \rho_i) \phi_k.

