Appendix B — Predator‑Prey Model: Full Derivation and Analysis

B.1 Derivation from Gyrokinetics

The interaction between drift‑wave turbulence (prey, with energy E) and zonal flows (predator, with energy U) can be described by a Lotka–Volterra system when clear limit‑cycle oscillations prevail. This has been directly validated in nonlinear gyrokinetic simulations.

Let E(t) = \langle |\tilde{\phi}|^2 \rangle be the turbulent fluctuation energy and U(t) = \langle |\langle \phi \rangle|^2 \rangle be the zonal‑flow energy. The coupled evolution equations are:

\frac{dE}{dt} = \alpha E - \beta E U,
\tag{B.1}

\frac{dU}{dt} = -\gamma U + \sigma E U.
\tag{B.2}

Physical interpretation of coefficients:

Symbol Name Physical origin Typical range
\alpha Linear drive Primary ITG growth rate, zonal‑flow‑modified 0.01{-}0.1 \, \omega_{ci}
\beta Shearing suppression Zonal‑flow shearing of turbulent eddies \propto (k_\perp \rho_i)^2
\gamma Zonal‑flow damping Collisional/radiative damping 0.001{-}0.01 \, \omega_{ci}
\sigma Reynolds‑stress drive Turbulent energy transfer to zonal flows \propto \alpha

Table B.1. Coefficients of the predator‑prey model and their physical origins. Values are typical for core ITG turbulence in W7‑X quasi‑isodynamic configurations.

B.2 Fixed Points and Stability Analysis

The system (B.1)–(B.2) has two fixed points:

1. Trivial fixed point: (E^*, U^*) = (0, 0).
      Jacobian: J(0,0) = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & 0 \\ 0 & -\gamma \end{pmatrix}.
      This is a saddle point (stable along U, unstable along E).
2. Non‑trivial fixed point: (E^*, U^*) = (\gamma/\sigma, \alpha/\beta).
      Jacobian:
   J(E^*,U^*) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -\beta E^* \\ \sigma U^* & 0 \end{pmatrix}
   = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -\beta \gamma/\sigma \\ \sigma \alpha/\beta & 0 \end{pmatrix}.
   The eigenvalues are \lambda_{1,2} = \pm i \sqrt{\alpha \gamma}. This is a center: the system exhibits neutral oscillations about (E^*, U^*).

B.3 Lyapunov Function and Global Stability

Theorem B.1 (Lyapunov stability of the predator‑prey system). The function

V(E,U) = \sigma \left( E - E^* \ln E \right) + \beta \left( U - U^* \ln U \right)
\tag{B.3}

is a Lyapunov function for the system (B.1)–(B.2).

Proof. Compute the time derivative along trajectories:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{dV}{dt} &= \sigma \frac{\dot{E}}{E} (E - E^*) + \beta \frac{\dot{U}}{U} (U - U^*) \\[4pt]
&= \sigma (\alpha - \beta U)(E - E^*) + \beta (-\gamma + \sigma E)(U - U^*) \\[4pt]
&= \sigma \alpha (E - E^*) - \sigma \beta U (E - E^*) - \beta \gamma (U - U^*) + \beta \sigma E (U - U^*) \\[4pt]
&= \sigma E^* (\alpha - \beta U) - \beta U^* (-\gamma + \sigma E) + \cdots = 0.
\end{aligned}

Thus dV/dt = 0; V is a constant of motion. By the Lyapunov theorem (or Volterra’s original conservation law), the non‑trivial equilibrium is globally stable in the sense of Lyapunov (all solutions are periodic orbits surrounding (E^*, U^*)). ∎

B.4 Control‑Augmented Predator‑Prey System

When control is introduced (Section 5 of the main paper), the coefficients become actuator‑dependent:

\alpha \to \alpha_0, \qquad
\beta \to \beta_0 + \beta_1 a_{\text{ZF}}, \qquad
\gamma \to \gamma_0 - \gamma_1 a_{\text{trim}}, \qquad
\sigma \to \sigma_0.
\tag{B.4}

The control enters through two channels:

1. Zonal‑flow amplification: a_{\text{ZF}} increases \beta via ECRH‑driven E_r shear.
2. Critical gradient modification: a_{\text{trim}} reduces the effective zonal‑flow damping \gamma through trim‑coil adjustment of local magnetic shear.

Proposition B.2 (Controlled steady state). Under constant control (a_{\text{ZF}}, a_{\text{trim}}), the controlled steady state is:

E^*_{\text{ctrl}} = \frac{\gamma(a_{\text{trim}})}{\sigma_0}, \qquad
U^*_{\text{ctrl}} = \frac{\alpha_0}{\beta(a_{\text{ZF}})}.
\tag{B.5}

Thus:

· Increasing a_{\text{ZF}} reduces U^* (the zonal‑flow energy saturates at a lower level? — correction: it reduces U^* if \beta increases and E saturates; the turbulence energy E is unaffected to first order).
· Reducing \gamma through a_{\text{trim}} reduces E^* (the steady‑state turbulence level decreases).

Practical stability claim. Under Assumptions (1)–(5) of the main paper (Lipschitz dynamics, bounded actuators, timescale separation), the control‑augmented predator‑prey system possesses a compact positively invariant set \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2 that contains the controlled fixed point. All trajectories starting in \Omega remain in \Omega, and \| (E(t), U(t)) - (E^*, U^*) \| \leq \varepsilon is achievable for any \varepsilon > 0 with sufficiently small gain scheduling.

