Appendix C — Fourier Neural Operator: Architecture and Approximation Theory

C.1 FNO Architecture

The Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) is a neural network architecture that learns mappings between infinite‑dimensional function spaces. The NTCS surrogate G_\phi uses an FNO backbone.

Layer structure: An FNO consists of:

1. Lifting layer: P: \mathbb{R}^{d_z} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_v} (linear).
2. L Fourier layers: Each layer F_\ell: \mathbb{R}^{d_v} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_v} applies:
   F_\ell(v) = \sigma \left( W_\ell v + \mathcal{F}^{-1} \left( R_\ell \cdot \mathcal{F}(v) \right) \right),
   \tag{C.1}
   where:
   · \mathcal{F} is the discrete Fourier transform,
   · R_\ell \in \mathbb{C}^{k_{\max} \times d_v \times d_v} is a learnable weight tensor in Fourier space,
   · W_\ell is a local linear transformation,
   · \sigma is the GELU activation.
3. Projection layer: Q: \mathbb{R}^{d_v} \to \mathbb{R}^{d_z} (linear).

NTCS‑specific parameters:

· Latent dimension d_z = 128.
· Hidden dimension d_v = 256.
· Number of Fourier layers L = 4.
· Maximum Fourier modes k_{\max} = 64 in each spatial direction.
· Actuator conditioning via FiLM (Feature‑wise Linear Modulation).

C.2 Universal Approximation Theorem

Theorem C.1 (Universal approximation of FNOs). Let \mathcal{G}: \mathcal{H}^s(\mathbb{T}^d) \to \mathcal{H}^{s'}(\mathbb{T}^d) be a continuous operator between Sobolev spaces, and let \varepsilon > 0. There exists an FNO \mathcal{G}_\theta with depth L and mode cutoff k_{\max} such that:

\sup_{\|u\|_{\mathcal{H}^s} \leq 1} \|\mathcal{G}(u) - \mathcal{G}_\theta(u)\|_{\mathcal{H}^{s'}} \leq \varepsilon.
\tag{C.2}

Moreover, for operators associated with elliptic PDEs (e.g., Darcy flow) and the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations, the required number of Fourier modes k_{\max} grows sub‑log‑linearly with the reciprocal error 1/\varepsilon.

Corollary C.2 (Application to gyrokinetics). Let \mathcal{N}: \mathcal{F}_{\text{gyro}} \to \mathcal{F}_{\text{gyro}} be the projected gyrokinetic operator in latent space. Under Assumptions (1)–(6), there exists an FNO G_\phi such that:

\|G_\phi(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a}) - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a})\|_\infty \leq \varepsilon,
\tag{C.3}

with k_{\max} = \mathcal{O}(\log(1/\varepsilon)) provided the Green’s function of the linearised gyrokinetic operator exhibits spectral decay of order s > d/2. This spectral decay has been verified in GX simulations of ITG turbulence in W7‑X geometry.

C.3 Error Propagation in Multi‑Step Rollouts

Theorem C.3 (Error bound for autoregressive rollout). Let G_\phi satisfy (C.3) with Lipschitz constant L_G. Then for an H-step rollout from initial condition \mathbf{z}_0:

\|\mathbf{z}_H^{\text{pred}} - \mathbf{z}_H^{\text{true}}\| \leq \varepsilon \cdot \frac{L_G^H - 1}{L_G - 1}.
\tag{C.4}

If L_G < 1 (contractive dynamics, typical for saturated turbulence near marginal stability), the error remains bounded by \varepsilon / (1 - L_G) for all H. If L_G \geq 1, the error grows at most exponentially with Lyapunov exponent \lambda = \ln(L_G).

Proof. Standard Grönwall inequality applied to the difference equation \mathbf{e}_{t+1} = G_\phi(\mathbf{z}_t^{\text{pred}}) - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z}_t^{\text{true}}) + \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z}_t^{\text{pred}}) - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z}_t^{\text{true}}). Bounding the first term by \varepsilon and the second by L_G \|\mathbf{e}_t\| gives the geometric series. ∎

