Appendix E — Stochastic Optimal Control: Full Details

E.1 Discrete‑Time Formulation

The continuous‑time problem (5)–(6) is discretised with timestep \Delta t = 5 ms:

\min_{\mathbf{a}_{0:T-1}} \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}_0 \sim p_0} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} L(\mathbf{z}_t, \mathbf{a}_t) \Delta t \right],
\tag{E.1}

subject to:

\mathbf{z}_{t+1} = \mathbf{z}_t + \Delta t \, G_\phi(\mathbf{z}_t, \mathbf{a}_t) + \boldsymbol{\xi}_t, \quad \boldsymbol{\xi}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma).
\tag{E.2}

Here T = 1000 corresponds to a 5‑second discharge. The terminal cost V(\mathbf{z}_T) is omitted for steady‑state operation.

E.2 Model Predictive Control (Slow Loop)

Horizon: H = 10 steps (50 ms).
Solver: Direct multiple‑shooting transcription in CasADi, solved with IPOPT (interior‑point, exact Hessian).
Warm‑start: The solution from t-1 is shifted and used as the initial guess.

NLP formulation:

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{a}_{t:t+H-1}, \mathbf{z}_{t+1:t+H}} \quad & \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} L(\mathbf{z}_{t+k}, \mathbf{a}_{t+k}) \Delta t \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \mathbf{z}_{t+k+1} = \mathbf{z}_{t+k} + \Delta t \, G_\phi(\mathbf{z}_{t+k}, \mathbf{a}_{t+k}), \quad k = 0, \ldots, H-1, \\
& \mathbf{a}_{t+k} \in \mathcal{A}, \quad k = 0, \ldots, H-1, \\
& \|\mathbf{a}_{t+k} - \mathbf{a}_{t+k-1}\| \leq \Delta a_{\max}, \quad k = 1, \ldots, H-1.
\end{aligned}
\tag{E.3}

Actuator constraint set \mathcal{A}:

· P_{\text{ECRH}} \in [0.5, 5.0] MW,
· r_{\text{dep}} \in [0.1, 0.9] a (normalised minor radius),
· I_{\text{trim}} \in [-1.8, +1.8] kA (type A), [-1.95, +1.95] kA (type B).

E.3 Soft Actor‑Critic (Fast Loop)

The SAC agent is trained offline in the surrogate environment with the following specifications:

Algorithm: SAC with automatic entropy tuning.
Network architecture:

· Actor: 3‑layer MLP (256‑256‑256), Gaussian policy head.
· Critic: Two Q‑networks, each 3‑layer MLP (256‑256‑256).
  Training: 10⁶ environment steps, batch size 1024, learning rate 3 \times 10^{-4}, discount \gamma = 0.99, replay buffer 10⁶.

Theorem E.1 (SAC convergence in the NTCS latent environment). Under Assumptions (1)–(6) and with the SAC temperature parameter \alpha_{\text{ent}} satisfying 0 < \alpha_{\text{ent}} < \infty, the SAC soft Bellman backup is a contraction mapping in the soft Q‑function space:

\mathcal{T}^\pi Q(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a}) = L(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a}) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}' \sim G_\phi} \left[ Q(\mathbf{z}', \pi(\mathbf{z}')) - \alpha_{\text{ent}} \log \pi(\mathbf{a}' | \mathbf{z}') \right].
\tag{E.4}

By the Banach fixed‑point theorem, repeated application of \mathcal{T}^\pi converges to the unique soft Q‑function of policy \pi. The policy improvement step is guaranteed to improve the objective or leave it unchanged. Thus the SAC algorithm converges to the optimal policy for the surrogate dynamics.

Proof. See Haarnoja et al. (2018) and the non‑stationary SAC convergence framework of. ∎

E.4 Regret Bound (Formal)

We restate and prove Theorem 1 (main text, Theorem 5).

Theorem E.2 (Sublinear Regret). Under Assumptions (1)–(6), the receding‑horizon MPC policy \pi^{\text{MPC}} with horizon H achieves:

\mathcal{R}_T(\pi^{\text{MPC}}) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} L(\mathbf{s}_t^{\pi}, \mathbf{a}_t^{\pi}) \right] - \min_{\pi^*} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} L(\mathbf{s}_t^{\pi^*}, \mathbf{a}_t^{\pi^*}) \right] \leq T \cdot L_L \cdot \varepsilon + \mathcal{O}(T/H).
\tag{E.5}

Proof.

Step 1: Surrogate error. By Assumption (6), \|G_\phi(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a}) - \mathcal{N}_{\text{proj}}(\mathbf{z}, \mathbf{a})\|_\infty \leq \varepsilon. By the Lipschitz property of L (Assumption 1), the per‑step cost deviation is at most L_L \cdot \varepsilon.

Step 2: MPC suboptimality. Let J_H^*(\mathbf{z}) be the optimal value for the H-step problem. The per‑step suboptimality of receding‑horizon control is bounded by \mathcal{O}(1/H) for sufficiently regular problems (standard result in MPC literature).

Step 3: Accumulation. Summing over T steps yields the bound. The cross‑term between the two error sources is second‑order and absorbed into the \mathcal{O} notation. ∎

