Appendix F — Conformal Safety Filter

F.1 SODA‑MPC Integration

We adopt the SODA‑MPC framework for out‑of‑distribution (OOD) detection and safe fallback.

Ensemble: Train N_{\text{ens}} = 5 independent FNO surrogates \{G_{\phi_i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{ens}}} on bootstrap‑resampled training subsets.

OOD signal: At each timestep, compute the one‑step ensemble prediction \{\mathbf{z}_{t+1}^{(i)}\}. The OOD signal is:

s_t = \sigma_{\max}\left( \text{Cov}\left( \{\mathbf{z}_{t+1}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{ens}}} \right) \right),
\tag{F.1}

the maximum singular value of the empirical covariance matrix.

Calibration: Conformal prediction provides a threshold \tau(\delta) such that, with user‑specified confidence 1 - \delta:

\mathbb{P}(s_t > \tau(\delta) \mid \text{in‑distribution}) \leq \delta.
\tag{F.2}

The threshold is computed from a held‑out calibration set of 5,000 in‑distribution trajectories.

Fallback control: When s_t > \tau(\delta) (OOD detected):

· Fast loop: Hold last safe action a_{t-1}.
· Slow loop: Switch to worst‑case zonal‑flow damping policy:
  a_{\text{ZF}} \leftarrow a_{\text{ZF}}^{\max}, \quad a_{\text{trim}} \leftarrow 0.

This guarantees (probabilistically) that the plasma remains within a pre‑computed safe operating region.

F.2 Safety‑Critical MPC via Conformalised System Level Synthesis

For rigorous safety guarantees, the conformal prediction framework can be integrated with system‑level synthesis (SLS) to provide robust OOD control with coverage and robustness guarantees under distributional drift. However, for the present paper, the ensemble‑based SODA‑MPC approach suffices.

